// Picaresque: original stories written for this game. Format: docs/v3/BLUEPRINT-FORMAT.md.

== picaresque.f01 | feature | journey
title: The Fourth Man
genre: picaresque + adventure
tone: Sun-struck, wry and wide-eyed
era: 1528 | period
setting: The Gulf coast, the deserts and the mountains of a continent no outsider has crossed | real | wilderness
logline: A Moroccan slave shipwrecked with three Spanish gentlemen on an unknown shore becomes, over eight years and six thousand miles on foot, the one the others cannot live without: the talker, the trader, the healer who goes ahead.
premise: Three hundred men landed to conquer a province. Four are left: three hidalgos and the slave of one of them, {said}, who was sold as a boy in a famine and has been a Christian on paper ever since. The gentlemen know how to command. He knows how to learn a language in a week, read a stranger's face, and make himself useful to whoever has the food.
synopsis: The story is told as picaresque: episodes strung on a road, a servant's eye on his masters, survival by wit. The raft comes ashore on an island the survivors name for bad luck. Episode one: slavery turned upside down. The islanders, starving themselves, enslave the Spaniards to dig roots; {said}, who has been a slave already, knows how it is done and does it better. Episode two: the trade. He walks the coast for a shell-dealer and learns six tongues and that there are no cities of gold, only people. Episode three: the cure. Ordered to heal a sick man or go hungry, his master's friend {alonso} makes the sign of the cross and breathes on him; the man recovers. They are now medicine men. {said} sees the business in it before any of them and becomes its manager: he goes ahead to each village with a gourd rattle to announce the children of the sun, negotiates the gifts, and translates the blessing. Episode four: the walk west, with a following of hundreds who pass them from nation to nation, plundering each village joyfully in their name. He is no longer anybody's servant in practice; in the gentlemen's journals he is still "the black". He has a wife in one country and friends in thirty. Episode five: the reunion. In the eighth year they meet horsemen, slave raiders, their own people; the following is nearly taken in chains. The gentlemen go to the capital and are received by the viceroy; {alonso} sails home to write the book. {said} is sold again, to the viceroy, since he was never anything else on paper. The last episode is the picaro's revenge on a small scale and his end on a large one: sent north as guide to a friar, he goes days ahead as he always did, with rattle, greyhounds and an entourage, sending back crosses whose size is supposed to signal the riches he finds. He sends back a cross as tall as a man, and walks on, into a city of stone, and out of the written record. The film gives three endings told by three peoples, and prefers the one in which he was last seen walking away.
question: Who is the hero of a journey: the man who writes it or the man who makes it possible?
themes: the servant as the competent one; identity as a survival skill; whose account survives
audience: Adults and older teenagers who like big journeys told with irony
viewpoint: {said}'s, in voice-over that corrects his masters' book as it goes
stakes: Life from day to day, and whether a man with no legal self can make one
conflict: A slave's wit against a continent, his masters' pride, and the paper that says what he is
ending: They cross. The gentlemen are honoured. He is sold again, sent north, and walks out of history, possibly on purpose.
experience: A grin, a sense of the earth's size, real anger at a ledger entry, and a mysterious, freeing last image
look: Salt flats, cane huts, buffalo plains, red canyons; four ragged figures growing browner and thinner; a gourd rattle and feathers
sound: A dozen languages learned on screen; a rattle; the dry voice of a man who has been underestimated for a living
m: belong | system | belonging-vs-identity | open
@ said | Said of Azemmour | lead | protagonist, narrator | human | he/him | 25 | To be alive tomorrow, and nobody's | He was sold for bread at twelve | The paper follows him across the world | Quick, charming, ironic, patient as only a slave learns to be | From property to indispensable to legend | Fast, funny; learns a tongue a week | Rags, then feathers, bells and a gourd rattle
@ alonso | Don Alonso | co-lead | foil | human | he/him | 38 | To survive, and then to be understood | He came to conquer and stayed to heal | He will write the book and leave things out | Devout, tough, honest by his lights | From conqueror to something kinder, on the page at least | Formal Castilian, prayers | A rosary, a beard to the chest
@ gaspar | Don Gaspar | supporting | antagonist | human | he/him | 30 | To get home a gentleman | He owns {said} | He cannot do anything {said} can | Proud, resentful, dependent | From master to a seller | Orders, complaints | The remains of a doublet
@ the_trader | The Shell Trader | supporting | mentor | human | she/her | 50 | Good shells and a good walker | She trades between nations at war | The newcomer learns too fast | Shrewd, amused, unsparing | From owner to the one who teaches him the roads | A coast language, laughter | Shell necklaces, a carrying net
~ said - gaspar | slave and master | property | property again, by a signature
~ said - alonso | servant and healer | he carries his water | he runs his miracles
~ said - the_trader | captive and merchant | a walker she bought | the roads he will use for eight years
# The Island | EXT | Barrier Island Beach | DAWN | said, alonso, gaspar | Come ashore | A raft of hides breaks in the surf. Eighty men crawl out. By spring there are fifteen. The gentlemen wait for orders. {said} goes to see who lives here | Shipwrecked | 6 | water, crowd=80, stunts=raft wreck, vfx=storm surf
# The Island | EXT | Root Marsh | DAY | said, gaspar | Dig | The islanders set the Spaniards to dig roots under water till their fingers bleed. {said} shows his master how to stand so the back lasts. His master does not thank him | | 5 | water, crowd=40
# The Coast | EXT | Coastal Trail | DAY | said, the_trader | Trade | Shells inland, hides and red ochre back. A new language at each river. She tells him there is no gold. He says there never is | He learns the roads | 6 | crowd=30, props=shells+carrying net
# The Cure | INT | Brush Shelter | NIGHT | said, alonso, gaspar | Heal or starve | A sick man, a hungry village, an ultimatum. {alonso} prays and breathes on him. In the morning the man walks. {said} is already counting what the village brings | They become healers | 6 | crowd=40
# The Walk | EXT | Desert Village | DAY | said, alonso, gaspar | Go ahead | He arrives first with the rattle and announces the children of the sun. He settles the gifts. Six hundred followers come over the ridge behind the three thin Spaniards | He runs the miracle | 7 | crowd=600, props=gourd rattle, vfx=desert column
# The Walk | EXT | Mountain Pass | DUSK | said, alonso | Be asked | {alonso} asks, for the first time in six years, what he wants. He says: to keep walking. {alonso} does not write it down | | 4 | vfx=mountain vista
# The Riders | EXT | River Plain | DAY | said, alonso, gaspar | Meet their own | Horsemen: slave raiders, countrymen. They stare at four savages speaking Castilian. They eye the six hundred as stock. The gentlemen weep for joy. {said} does not | They are found | 6 | crowd=300, animals=horses
# The Riders | INT | Viceroy's Palace, Anteroom | DAY | said, gaspar | Be sold | A signature, a price. His master cannot meet his eye. On paper nothing has happened in eight years | He is sold again | 5 | props=bill of sale
# North | EXT | High Desert Road | DAY | said | Send back a cross | Days ahead of the friar, with bells, greyhounds, an escort of three hundred. A cross as tall as a man goes back down the trail. He looks at a city of stone on a hill and walks toward it | | 5 | crowd=300, animals=greyhounds, vfx=pueblo on a mesa
review: authored | 2026-11-12 | written whole; drawn from the crossing of North America by four shipwreck survivors, 1528 to 1536, and the journey north in 1539, with invented names
